      "heading": "How does online therapy work, step by step?",
      "body": "<p><strong>From signup to your first session, online therapy follows the same arc on every major platform: a questionnaire, a match with a licensed therapist, scheduling, and then an ongoing rhythm of sessions and messaging.</strong> Here is what each step actually involves, so nothing surprises you.</p>\n<h3>Step 1: The intake questionnaire</h3>\n<p>You answer a structured set of questions - usually ten to twenty minutes - about what brings you to therapy, how you have been feeling, any previous therapy or medication, your preferences for a therapist (gender, age range, faith background, specialties, language), your availability, and your location. The location question is not marketing: it determines which clinicians are legally allowed to treat you. Some questionnaires include brief screening instruments for depression and anxiety; answer them honestly, because they shape the match and give your therapist a baseline.</p>\n<h3>Step 2: Matching</h3>\n<p>Platforms match you with a therapist based on your answers, their specialties, and licensing in your state. On betterhelp, the largest network, matching happens in as little as two days; some platforms let you browse and pick from a short list instead. The match is a starting point, not a verdict. Every platform in our coverage lets you switch therapists, and the community feedback we track says the second match is often the one that sticks. Treat switching as normal, not as failure.</p>\n<h3>Step 3: Choosing a format and schedule</h3>\n<p>You pick how you want to meet. Live video is closest to a traditional session. Phone suits people who find video draining or whose home lacks a private corner with good light. Live chat is typed conversation in real time. Messaging is asynchronous: you write when you want, and your therapist responds within their stated window, typically on a set number of days per week. Most platforms let you mix formats, and most people settle into a weekly live session plus messaging in between.</p>\n<h3>Step 4: The first session</h3>\n<p>The first session is an extended conversation about your history, your goals, and how you prefer to work. Expect your therapist to ask about your current symptoms, sleep, relationships, work, substance use, past treatment, and safety. Expect to talk about confidentiality and its limits, and about what happens in a crisis. You are also interviewing them: ask about their experience with your concern, the approach they use, and how they will measure progress. If something feels off, that is information. Our guide to <a href=\"/online-therapy-for-beginners\">online therapy for beginners</a> walks through this session in detail.</p>\n<h3>Step 5: The ongoing rhythm</h3>\n<p>Therapy works through repetition. Weekly sessions are the standard starting cadence; some people move to every other week as they stabilize. Between sessions, messaging lets you capture a thought while it is fresh or ask a quick question. Many therapists assign practice between sessions - a thought record, an exposure exercise, a conversation to attempt. Doing that work is where most of the change happens.</p>\n<h3>Step 6: Reviewing progress, switching, or finishing</h3>\n<p>Good therapy has checkpoints. Around session four to six, you and your therapist should be able to say what is better, what is not, and whether the approach fits. If it does not, switch. When your goals are met, you taper or stop, usually with a plan for what to do if things slip. Subscriptions can be paused or cancelled; read the terms before you start, because cancellation and refund rules are the most common source of complaints at every subscription company.</p>\n<h3>What you need on your side</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>A device with a camera and microphone for video, or any phone for audio.</li>\n<li>A stable connection - video needs more bandwidth than you think; a wired or strong Wi-Fi link avoids the frozen-face problem.</li>\n<li>A private space for the session. A parked car, a closed office, a walk with earbuds for phone sessions - people get creative, and therapists have seen it all.</li>\n<li>Headphones, which keep your therapist's voice private and reduce echo.</li>\n<li>Something to write with. Noting one takeaway per session compounds.</li>\n</ul>"
