      "heading": "Does online therapy actually work?",
      "body": "<p><strong>For the most common reasons people seek therapy - anxiety, depression, stress, relationship difficulties and adjustment to life changes - therapy delivered by video or phone is widely accepted as an effective way to receive evidence-based psychotherapy, and the professional bodies that set standards in the field treat telehealth as a legitimate mode of care rather than a lesser substitute.</strong> The American Psychological Association describes psychotherapy as an effective treatment across a wide range of concerns and publishes guidance on practicing it via telehealth; the National Institute of Mental Health lists psychotherapies, including cognitive behavioral therapy, among established treatments for depression and anxiety disorders. Both are linked in the sources at the end of this guide.</p>\n<p>Three honest qualifications belong next to that answer.</p>\n<h3>1. The active ingredient is the therapy, not the screen</h3>\n<p>What makes therapy work is a structured, evidence-based approach delivered by a competent clinician inside a trusting relationship, applied consistently over time. Video and phone carry all of that. What they do not change is the need for the ingredients themselves. A poorly matched therapist or a vague, unstructured approach will not work better online than it would in a room, and a good one will not work worse.</p>\n<h3>2. Consistency beats format</h3>\n<p>The single strongest predictor of benefit that you control is attendance. People who show up weekly, do the between-session work, and stick with a therapist long enough to build trust tend to improve; people who attend sporadically tend not to, whatever the format. Online therapy's real advantage is that it removes the reasons people stop: the commute, the scheduling fight, the waitlist. If the office version is the one you would skip, the online version is the one that works.</p>\n<h3>3. Some situations call for in-person or higher-intensity care</h3>\n<p>Online therapy is best matched to mild-to-moderate concerns. Severe or complex presentations - active psychosis, severe eating disorders, acute suicidality, some trauma presentations, situations where a clinician needs to observe you physically or coordinate closely with a medical team - may need in-person care, intensive outpatient programs, or hospitalization. A good online therapist will tell you this and help you find the right level of care. Our <a href=\"/articles/does-online-therapy-work\">guide to the evidence on online therapy</a> covers what the research does and does not show in more depth.</p>\n<h3>What about messaging-only therapy?</h3>\n<p>Asynchronous messaging is the newest format and the one with the least consensus. It suits people who process in writing, who want to reach their therapist between live sessions, or whose schedule makes live sessions hard. It is less suited as a sole format for people who need real-time dialogue, who struggle to express themselves in text, or whose concerns are more acute. Most clinicians, and most of the community feedback we track, treat messaging as a strong complement to live sessions rather than a replacement. If you choose a messaging-first plan, know your therapist's response schedule in advance so silence is never a surprise.</p>"
