      "heading": "Who is online therapy right for - and who should look elsewhere?",
      "body": "<p><strong>Online therapy fits most adults with mild-to-moderate mental-health concerns who want flexible, consistent access to a licensed therapist. It is a poor fit for emergencies, for severe or unstable conditions, and for anyone who cannot secure a private space to talk.</strong> The checklist below is the one we would run before signing up.</p>\n<h3>Online therapy is a strong fit if you:</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Are dealing with anxiety, low mood, stress, burnout, grief, relationship strain, a life transition, or a long-standing pattern you want to change.</li>\n<li>Live somewhere with few therapists, long waitlists, or none who match your needs or identity.</li>\n<li>Have a schedule - shift work, caregiving, travel, a new baby - that makes a fixed weekly office slot unrealistic.</li>\n<li>Find it easier to open up from your own space, or want the option of phone or messaging on hard days.</li>\n<li>Want a wider choice of therapist than your zip code offers: a specialist in your concern, a therapist who shares your language, faith or background.</li>\n<li>Have insurance that covers an online platform, or an employer benefit that includes one.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Look at other options first if you:</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Are in crisis.</strong> Call or text 988 in the US, or 911 in immediate danger. Online therapy platforms are explicit that they are not emergency services.</li>\n<li><strong>Have a severe or unstable condition</strong> that needs close monitoring, physical examination, or coordination between therapist, prescriber and medical team.</li>\n<li><strong>Need a court-ordered evaluation, a formal diagnostic assessment for disability or legal purposes, or testing for conditions like ADHD or autism.</strong> These usually require specialized in-person assessment; many platforms do not provide them.</li>\n<li><strong>Cannot get privacy.</strong> Therapy where someone might overhear is therapy where you hold back. If home is not private, consider phone sessions from a car or a walk, or an in-person option.</li>\n<li><strong>Are under 18.</strong> Age policies differ by platform and by state law on minors' consent to treatment. Check the policy before signing up, and expect a parent or guardian to be involved.</li>\n<li><strong>Need medication and the platform does not prescribe.</strong> Choose a platform with psychiatry on board, or plan for a separate prescriber from the start. The therapist-vs-psychiatrist section below explains how to decide.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>A note on couples and families</h3>\n<p>Couples and family therapy work online, with two caveats. Everyone needs to be in a place where they can speak freely - which sometimes means separate rooms on separate devices. And not every platform offers it: Talkspace has a dedicated couples plan at $109 per week; betterhelp focuses on individual therapy, so check each platform's couples options before signing up. Our guides to <a href=\"/online-family-therapy\">online family therapy</a> and <a href=\"/articles/online-couples-therapy\">online couples therapy</a> cover what to expect.</p>"
